    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1092">

                <pb facs="1004" n="992" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg992"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760d.61/401: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-11-03T18:00:00+03:00">November 3, 1939—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 3—3:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>859. My 853, November 2.<anchor n="72" xml:id="d1092-f1"/> I am informed that the
                    Finnish delegation is going to the Kremlin at 6 o’clock this afternoon. A member
                    of the Finnish Legation has just advised me that while the violent tone of the
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi> editorial will undoubtedly render the
                    negotiations still more difficult it does not in the opinion of the Finnish
                    delegation basically alter the situation. He added that according to the text of
                    the speech of the Finnish Foreign Minister as received by the Legation the words
                    which he is quoted as saying in the <hi rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi>
                    editorial in regard to Russian imperialism and the impossibility of Finland’s
                    accepting the Soviet proposals for the rectification of the frontier north of
                    Leningrad are a fabrication put into the Minister’s mouth by <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi>.</p>
                <p>In the light of the foregoing, I consider it disturbing that <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi> should have had recourse to such fabrication
                    since, while distortion of meaning and even misquotation are customary Soviet
                    tactics, it is rare that remarks capable of disproof are deliberately
                    invented.</p>
                <p>The <hi rendition="#italic">Izvestiya</hi> which today appeared even later than
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Pravda</hi> makes no reference to the Soviet-Finnish
                    negotiations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
